"""
A wall clock that says what time it is, and a monotonic clock that says how much time has passed.

- Both clocks read in nanoseconds, as a Nat64.
- The wall clock counts from the epoch and can be set backwards: it is for a time a person reads.
- wallNanos gives zero when the wall clock could not be read.
- The monotonic clock never goes backwards; only the difference of two readings means anything.
- A build without a monotonic clock says so through hasMonotonic, and monotonicNanos gives None.

    let stamp: Nat64 := wallNanos();
    case monotonicNanos() of
        when Some(value as start: Nat64) do
            work();
            report(start);
        when None do
            work();
    end case;
"""
pragma Targets(Names => "linux openbsd freebsd darwin wasm");
module Standard.Time is
    """
    Nanoseconds since the epoch, from the wall clock; zero if it could not
    be read.  It can go backwards: use it for a time a person reads, never
    to measure how long something took.
    No contract: a number the system or the build gives.
    """
    function wallNanos(): Nat64;

    """
    Whether this build has a clock that does not go backwards.  Ask it
    before relying on monotonicNanos.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function hasMonotonic(): Bool;

    """
    Nanoseconds from a clock that does not go backwards, or None where this
    build has none.  The epoch means nothing: only the difference of two
    readings does.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    function monotonicNanos(): Option[Nat64];
end module.
